  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Object Pronoun Combinations (DO + IO in One Word)",
      "content": "When you want to replace BOTH a direct and indirect object with pronouns, they combine into a SINGLE word. The indirect object pronoun (le/les) ALWAYS comes first, followed by the direct object pronoun (lo/la/los/las).\n\nWhen le or les is followed by lo, la, los, or las, they change to SE + DO pronoun. This is one of the most important rules in Spanish pronoun usage.\n\nCombinations:\n- me + lo = melo\n- me + la = mela\n- te + lo = telo\n- te + la = tela\n- le + lo → se lo\n- le + la → se la\n- les + lo → se lo\n- les + la → se la\n- nos + lo = noslo\n- os + lo = oslo (Spain only)\n\nPlacement rules:\n- BEFORE conjugated verb: 'Te lo doy.' (I give it to you.)\n- ATTACHED to infinitive: 'Voy a dártelo.' (I'm going to give it to you.)\n- ATTACHED to gerund: 'Estoy preparándolo.' (I'm preparing it.)\n\nWhen attached to an infinitive or gerund, add an accent mark to maintain proper stress.",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "IO + DO",
          "Combined Form",
          "Example",
          "Translation"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "te + lo",
            "te lo",
            "Te lo digo.",
            "I tell it to you."
          ],
          [
            "le + la",
            "se la",
            "Se la di.",
            "I gave it to him/her."
          ],
          [
            "me + lo",
            "me lo",
            "Me lo compró.",
            "He bought it for me."
          ],
          [
            "les + los",
            "se los",
            "Se los envié.",
            "I sent them to them."
          ],
          [
            "te + las",
            "te las",
            "Te las pondré.",
            "I'll put them on you."
          ],
          [
            "nos + lo",
            "nos lo",
            "Nos lo explica.",
            "He explains it to us."
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        "¿Me lo puedes prestar? (Can you lend it to me?)",
        "Se la recomendé al médico. (I recommended it to the doctor.)",
        "Te lo voy a decir ahora mismo. (I'm going to tell it to you right now.)",
        "Nos los compró en el mercado. (He bought them for us at the market.)"
      ],
      "aid_note": "Romanian combines object pronouns similarly: 'mi-l' (it to me), 'ți-l' (it to you), 'i-l' (it to him/her). Spanish 'se lo' is equivalent to Romanian 'i-l'. The Romanian system is actually more similar to English word-for-word than Spanish."
    }
  ],
  "pronunciation": [
    {
      "title": "Accent Marks with Attached Pronouns",
      "content": "When object pronouns are attached to an infinitive or gerund, an accent mark is often needed to maintain the original stress:\n\n- dar + lo = darlo (2 syllables, stress on da) — no accent needed\n- dar + te + lo = dártelo (3 syllables) — accent on the 'a' to maintain stress\n- explicar + lo = explicarlo (4 syllables, stress on pa) — no accent needed\n- explicando + lo = explicándolo (5 syllables) — accent on the 'a' to maintain stress\n- preparar + lo = prepararlo (4 syllables, stress on pa) — no accent needed\n- preparando + lo = preparándolo (5 syllables) — accent on the 'a'\n\nThe rule: if attaching pronouns pushes the stress to break the natural pattern, add an accent mark.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "decírtelo",
          "source": "de- CIR-te-lo (stress on CIR)"
        },
        {
          "target": "explicándolo",
          "source": "ex-pli-CÁN-do-lo (stress on CÁN)"
        },
        {
          "target": "dártelo",
          "source": "DÁR-te-lo (stress on DÁR)"
        },
        {
          "target": "preparándolo",
          "source": "pre-pa-RÁN-do-lo (stress on RÁN)"
        }
      ],
      "aid_note": "Romanian pronouns are separated by a hyphen (mi-l, îți-l), while Spanish fuses them into a single word. The accent mark rules in Spanish are more complex due to the fused form."
    }
  ],
  "exercises": [
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "What does 'se lo' replace in Spanish?",
      "options": [
        "me + lo",
        "le + lo",
        "te + lo",
        "nos + lo"
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "'Se lo' replaces 'le/les + lo/la/los/las'. When le/les comes before lo/la/los/las, it changes to 'se'."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: 'Yo ____ (dar) el libro a María. → Yo ____ lo doy.' (I give the book to María → I give it to her.)",
      "correct": "se",
      "explanation": "'Se lo doy' — 'le' changes to 'se' when followed by 'lo': le + lo = se lo."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: 'Voy a ____ (decir) la verdad a ti. → Voy a ____ decir.' (I'm going to tell it to you.)",
      "correct": "decírtelo",
      "explanation": "'Decírtelo' — pronouns attach to the infinitive: te + lo + decir → decírtelo. An accent is added to maintain the stress."
    },
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "Where do object pronouns go with a conjugated verb?",
      "options": [
        "After the verb",
        "Before the conjugated verb",
        "At the end of the sentence",
        "They don't go with conjugated verbs"
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "Object pronouns go BEFORE the conjugated verb: 'Te lo doy' (I give it to you)."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: '¿Me ____ (you) ____ (lo) ____ (decir)?' (Can you tell it to me?)",
      "correct": "lo puedes decírmelo",
      "explanation": "'¿Me lo puedes decir?' — pronouns go before the conjugated verb: me lo + puedes decir. Or with infinitive: ¿Puedes decírmelo? Both are correct."
    },
